WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.625
Reporting is where many agencies stopped,

2
00:00:02.625 --> 00:00:06.450
so it's worth being precise about
what CISA actually receives.

3
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:11.415
The directive allows continuous integration
with CISA's monitoring, or quarterly

4
00:00:11.415 --> 00:00:16.025
manual reporting, and CISA's implementation
guidance describes four routes.

5
00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:19.846
CISA-hosted ScubaConnect,
where CISA manages reporting

6
00:00:19.846 --> 00:00:22.887
after setup and results
feed the CDM dashboard.

7
00:00:22.887 --> 00:00:27.937
Self-hosted ScubaConnect, where the
agency runs it and keeps local copies.

8
00:00:27.937 --> 00:00:32.727
An agency-hosted integration, through
CLAW or TALON, with automated scans

9
00:00:32.727 --> 00:00:37.438
at least once a quarter, which takes
development work on the agency's side.

10
00:00:37.438 --> 00:00:41.121
Or manual upload to CyberScope,
quarterly or more often,

11
00:00:41.121 --> 00:00:43.763
from the most recent version of the tool.

12
00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:48.090
Whatever the route, the reported
artifact is the tool's own results file,

13
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.363
and agencies are told not to rename it.

14
00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:54.730
A deviation that's operationally required
appears in the output as Omitted,

15
00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:58.127
with its rationale, and the
guidance says omissions must be

16
00:00:58.127 --> 00:01:02.287
approved through the organization's
security risk management process.

17
00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:06.597
CISA also supplies a template for
tracking non-compliant policies,

18
00:01:06.597 --> 00:01:08.563
planned fixes and target dates.

19
00:01:08.563 --> 00:01:11.825
In effect, it's a plan of
action and milestones.

20
00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:15.113
The directive puts the risk
decision in the right place.

21
00:01:15.113 --> 00:01:20.488
Authorizing officials may accept risk for
deviations from the mandatory policies to

22
00:01:20.488 --> 00:01:23.250
meet operational needs,
under agency policy,

23
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.237
and CISA reviews and
resolves what they submit.

24
00:01:26.237 --> 00:01:27.687
Two decisions follow.

25
00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:31.876
The CISO picks the reporting route,
and the assessment operators run it.

26
00:01:31.876 --> 00:01:35.612
Operators never edit results,
and never decide omissions.

27
00:01:35.612 --> 00:01:40.111
And every deviation without an end
date goes to the authorizing official,

28
00:01:40.111 --> 00:01:43.725
because a standing omission is
a standing risk acceptance.

29
00:01:43.725 --> 00:01:46.260
The reporting floor is at least quarterly,

30
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.440
so both are needed before
the next quarterly report.
